---
title: "TESSERA v1.1: more stable embeddings, fewer artefacts"
date: 2026-06-02
tags: [announcement, release]
author: "Anil Madhavapeddy and Frank Feng on behalf of team TESSERA"
description: "v1.1 embeddings are more temporally stable year-on-year and have far fewer artefacts in low-observation areas, and nothing in your workflow has to change."
---

We're pleased to announce the availability of TESSERA v1.1, a retrained
successor to our original v1.0 model.  If you use TESSERA embeddings for any
downstream tasks such as classification, change detection, regression, then the
new embeddings are drop-in replacements that will improve accuracy, with no
changes needed in your workflow.

## What's better for your downstream tasks

- More **temporal stability** year-on-year. v1.1 128-dim embeddings are more
  consistent across years for the same location. That makes multi-year work such
  as change detection, trend analysis, or training a classifier on one year and
  applying it to another, much more reliable.
- **Fewer artefacts in low-observation areas.** v1.0 could sometimes produce noisy
  tiles where a pixel with few clear satellite observations (due to persistent
  cloud, short time series, or sensor gaps). v1.1 handles sparse observations
  gracefully, so quality holds up in the places that formerly showed issues.
  Note that this only affected large-scale downstream tasks; if you used a small
  ROI that didn't straddle problematic tiles, there will be no change.
- **Improved coastal coverage** v1.0's land mask was too aggressive and dropped
  legitimate land pixels in coastal areas. We've listened to our mangrove-loving
  friends and significantly extended the training and inference buffer to cover
  coastlines and remote islands.

